</table>

<p>The last entry — the custom RSS recipe — is the secret weapon. Calibre allows you to create a custom news recipe from any RSS or Atom feed. If your favorite blog does not have a built-in recipe, paste its RSS feed URL into a custom recipe, and Calibre will fetch, format, and deliver new posts as EPUB automatically.</p>

<h3 id="custom-rss-recipe-calibre">Creating a Custom RSS Recipe in Calibre</h3>

<p>To set up a custom RSS feed in Calibre:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Open Calibre and click <em>Fetch news → Add custom news source</em>.</li>
  <li>Give your recipe a name (e.g., "My Blog Roll").</li>
  <li>Under "Feed URL," paste the RSS or Atom feed URL of the blog or news site you want to follow.</li>
  <li>Set the schedule — daily, weekly, or on specific days.</li>
  <li>Click OK. Calibre will test the feed and confirm formatting.</li>
</ol>

<p>You can add multiple feed URLs to a single custom recipe. Calibre will combine all articles from all feeds into one unified EPUB, sorted by date. This is ideal for creating a "morning digest" that pulls from your favorite five tech blogs, three news sites, and two hobby newsletters — all in one file.</p>

<h2 id="inkfeed-rss-kindle">Method 2: Inkfeed — A Modern RSS Reader for Kindle</h2>

<p>Inkfeed is a dedicated RSS-to-Kindle service that launched in mid-2026. Unlike Calibre's desktop-bound approach, Inkfeed is cloud-based: you subscribe to feeds on its website, and it pushes formatted EPUB editions to your Kindle email address automatically.</p>

<p>The setup takes under two minutes:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Go to Inkfeed's website and sign up (free tier supports up to 10 feeds).</li>
  <li>Add RSS feed URLs — Inkfeed fetches them on a configurable schedule (daily or every 6 hours).</li>
  <li>Enter your Kindle email address (<a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="740d1b01341f1d1a1018115a171b19">[email&#160;protected]</a>) and authorize @inkfeed.com as an approved sender in your Amazon account.</li>
  <li>That is it — new articles are formatted as EPUB and delivered to your Kindle every morning.</li>
</ol>

<p>Inkfeed's EPUB formatting is clean and e-reader-optimized: no ads, responsive images, proper heading hierarchy, and a generated table of contents. The main advantage over Calibre is that you do not need a computer running 24/7 — the service handles everything in the cloud.</p>

<h2 id="send-to-kindle-web">Method 3: Send to Kindle for Individual Articles</h2>

<p>For the occasional article rather than a daily news batch, Amazon's "Send to Kindle" browser extension and email service are the quickest option. Available as a Chrome and Firefox extension, Send to Kindle lets you push any web page to your Kindle with one click.</p>

<p>Amazon processes the page server-side: it strips clutter, reformats the text for e-ink screens, and delivers the article wirelessly to your Kindle within 60 seconds. The result is a well-formatted, ad-free article with embedded images and hyperlinks preserved.</p>

<p>The same functionality is available via email. Send an email with the article URL — or the full article text — to your Kindle's @kindle.com address, and Amazon's pipeline will convert it and push it to your device. You can find your Kindle email address under <em>Manage Your Content and Devices → Preferences → Personal Document Settings</em>.</p>

<p>This method is best for a <em>small number</em> of long-form articles you want to read later, not for bulk news consumption. For daily news, the Calibre or Inkfeed approaches are more efficient.</p>

<h2 id="android-ereader-browser">Method 4: On-Device Browser (Android E-Readers)</h2>

<p>If you own an Android-based e-reader like a Boox (Onyx), Meebook, or Bigme, you already have a web browser and RSS reader apps available. The advantage of Android e-readers is that you can install any RSS reader from the Google Play Store — Feedly, Inoreader, NetNewsWire, or any of dozens of dedicated E Ink-optimized RSS apps.</p>

<p>The key consideration with Android e-readers is E Ink display refresh. Standard RSS reader apps are designed for LCD screens and may have animations, scrolling flicker, or small touch targets that are awkward on E Ink. Look for apps with "E Ink mode" or "night mode" — they switch to dark text on a light background and reduce or eliminate animations. Some popular options in 2026 include:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Feedly</strong> — Web-based RSS with a clean, minimal interface that works well on E Ink in reader mode</li>
  <li><strong>Inoreader</strong> — Heavyweight RSS with offline reading, full-text extraction, and tagging</li>
  <li><strong>Read You</strong> — Open-source Android RSS reader with Material You design and E Ink-friendly themes</li>
  <li><strong>Fluent Reader</strong> — Lightweight RSS aggregator with an uncluttered reading view</li>
</ul>

<p>For non-Android e-readers (Kindle, Kobo, PocketBook), the on-device browser is functional but limited. Kobo's browser works for basic web pages but struggles with modern JavaScript-heavy news sites. PocketBook's browser is slightly more capable thanks to its Linux-based architecture. The browser-on-e-reader experience is best reserved for reading text-heavy pages without complex layouts.</p>

<h2 id="comparison">Method Comparison: Which One Should You Use?</h2>

