<p>Our recommendation for a daily news habit: set up Calibre Fetch News with 3-5 recipes (one major newspaper, one tech site, one niche interest blog) and schedule it for 6:00 AM. If you do not want to keep a computer running, Inkfeed provides the same convenience from the cloud. Supplement with Send to Kindle for articles you discover during the day that fall outside your regular feed rotation.</p>

<h2 id="tips-news-ereader">Tips for the Best Reading Experience</h2>

<p>Regardless of which method you choose, these tweaks will improve your news-reading experience on an e-reader:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Increase font size slightly</strong> — News articles tend to have shorter paragraphs and more subheadings than novels. A slightly larger font makes scanning easier.</li>
  <li><strong>Enable landscape mode</strong> — For multi-column newspaper layouts or articles with wide tables, landscape mode on your e-reader can make the content much more readable.</li>
  <li><strong>Use the dictionary</strong> — News reading exposes you to more varied vocabulary than novels. Long-press unfamiliar words to look them up. Calibre's news recipes even embed the default dictionary.</li>
  <li><strong>Stick to weekly schedules for magazines</strong> — The Economist or The New Yorker are best fetched weekly, not daily. You do not need 200 pages of content every morning.</li>
  <li><strong>Prune your feeds quarterly</strong> — RSS feeds accumulate. Every three months, review your custom recipe's feed list and remove sources you never read. A focused 5-feed digests is more useful than a bloated 20-feed firehose.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<section class="faq" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">
  <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <h3 itemprop="name">Can I read news on a Kindle without a computer?</h3>
    <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
      <div itemprop="text">
        <p>Yes. The Inkfeed cloud service delivers RSS-formatted news directly to your Kindle via email — no computer needed. Amazon's Send to Kindle browser extension also works from your phone or tablet. For a completely computer-free setup, Inkfeed is the easiest option.</p>
      </div>
