  <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <h3 itemprop="name">Does Calibre Fetch News work with paywalled news sites?</h3>
    <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
      <div itemprop="text">
        <p>It depends on the site. Calibre's built-in recipes for paywalled publications like The Economist, The New York Times, and The Wall Street Journal include login fields — you can enter your subscription credentials in the recipe settings, and Calibre will authenticate before downloading. For sites without a recipe that require a login, you can try the custom RSS approach, but the result may include only headlines and summaries rather than full articles.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <h3 itemprop="name">How many news articles can Calibre download at once?</h3>
    <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
      <div itemprop="text">
        <p>Calibre does not enforce a hard limit on article count per recipe, but in practice, most news recipes download between 20 and 100 articles per edition. The resulting EPUB file is typically 2-10 MB — well within the capacity of any modern e-reader. If a recipe produces more than 200 articles, you can limit it by editing the recipe's <code>max_articles_per_feed</code> setting in Calibre's advanced recipe editor.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <h3 itemprop="name">Can I read news on a Kobo like I can on a Kindle?</h3>
    <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
      <div itemprop="text">
        <p>Yes, and in some ways better. Kobo natively reads EPUB — the format Calibre's Fetch News produces — so there is no conversion step. You can sideload Calibre-generated news EPUBs via USB (drag and drop) or wirelessly using Calibre's content server. Kobo also has a built-in Pocket integration that syncs saved articles from the web. Some PocketBook models offer a built-in RSS reader in the firmware.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <h3 itemprop="name">Will reading news drain my e-reader battery faster?</h3>
    <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
      <div itemprop="text">
        <p>Not significantly. E Ink displays only consume power when turning pages, not while displaying static text. A daily news edition of 50-80 pages might require 100-200 page turns — that is roughly the same power draw as reading 5-10 novel chapters. Your e-reader battery should last weeks with daily news reading added to your routine. The larger battery drain comes from Wi-Fi sync (for cloud delivery methods), which you can minimize by scheduling syncs during charging.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <h3 itemprop="name">Are there free news RSS feeds in 2026?</h3>
    <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
      <div itemprop="text">
        <p>Many major news outlets still offer free RSS feeds, though some have restricted them in recent years. The BBC, The Guardian, Reuters, NPR, Ars Technica, WIRED, and most tech blogs still provide full-text RSS feeds at no cost. Substack newsletters and independent blogs almost universally offer RSS. For sites that have dropped RSS, services like Kill the Newsletter generate RSS feeds from email newsletters, keeping the old-school RSS ecosystem alive and well in 2026.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
